% VERIFIED against the book itself, 2026-10-09 (Jan supplied the PDF; literature/subscription/).
%
% The year is right and was nearly changed on worse evidence.  The copyright page reads
% "First published 1985 / First paperback edition 1990 / SECOND EDITION FIRST PUBLISHED 2013",
% copyright "1985, 2013".  Crossref's 2012-10-22 and Cambridge's own "20 December 2012" are
% release dates for this ISBN, not the edition's year; the Library of Congress line on the same
% page is "QA188.H66 2012".  Keep year = 2013.
%
% THE LOCATOR WAS WRONG, AND IN AN INSTRUCTIVE WAY.  The note here used to say "believed Theorem
% 8.4.5".  The NUMBER is right; what it says is not.  Theorem 8.4.5 is the EQUALITY case:
% "Let A, B in M_n.  Suppose that A is nonnegative and irreducible, and A >= |B|.  Let
% lambda = e^{i phi} rho(B) be a given maximum-modulus eigenvalue of B.  If rho(A) = rho(B), then
% there is a diagonal unitary matrix D in M_n such that B = e^{i phi} A D D^{-1}."  It is not a
% strict-comparison theorem and does not read like one out of context.
%
% Section 8.4 ("Irreducible nonnegative matrices", p. 533) contains ONLY Lemmas 8.4.1-8.4.3,
% Theorems 8.4.4-8.4.5 and Corollaries 8.4.6-8.4.7; none of them is the strict comparison.  The
% strict inequality appears in this book only as PROBLEM 8.2.P15(b), and only for B positive.
%
% So prop-perron-threshold now cites the pair that really does the work:
%   Corollary 8.1.19  "Let A, B in M_n be nonnegative.  If 0 <= A <= B, then rho(A) <= rho(B)."
%   Theorem   8.4.5   rules out equality: for nonnegative M, M' it forces M = M' entrywise,
%                     since |M[i][j]| = |M'[i][j]| and both are nonnegative.
% Also on hand if ever wanted: Theorem 8.1.18 (the |A| <= B version), Theorem 8.2.8 (Perron),
% Theorem 8.3.1 (rho is an eigenvalue, nonnegative case), Theorem 8.4.4 (Perron-Frobenius).
@book{HornJohnson2013,
  author    = {Horn, Roger A. and Johnson, Charles R.},
  title     = {Matrix Analysis},
  edition   = {2},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address   = {Cambridge},
  year      = {2013},
  isbn      = {978-0-521-83940-2},
  note      = {Cited in article 2 at prop-perron-threshold, for Corollary 8.1.19 and
               Theorem 8.4.5; both checked against the book 2026-10-09}
}

